{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Value the Steering Wheel: Control Premium in IT Services M&A",
  "scenario": "You are analysing a potential acquisition of Mindtree-like IT services companies for a strategic buyer. Your Mumbai desk has compiled a precedent-transactions table of recent Indian IT services M&A (2018–2024). You must compute control premiums, transaction multiples, and use them to value a hypothetical target company in a control scenario.",
  "instructions": "Build a precedent-transactions analysis: compute each deal's control premium and transaction EV/EBITDA multiple, find the median transaction multiple across all deals, then apply it to value a mystery IT services target. Compare the implied control price to the trading-comps baseline.",
  "learning_objective": "Compute control premiums and transaction multiples from real M&A deals, understand why strategic acquirers pay more than market price, and apply precedent multiples to value a company in a control context.",
  "hints": [
    "Control Premium = (Offer Price − Market Price Before Deal) / Market Price Before Deal. Compute this for each row, then look for the typical 20–40% range.",
    "Transaction EV/EBITDA = Deal Enterprise Value / EBITDA at deal date. You'll need to sum Equity Value and Net Debt to get EV. Then divide by EBITDA.",
    "For the target valuation: Implied EV = Median Transaction Multiple × Target EBITDA. Then Implied Equity = Implied EV − Target Net Debt. Then divide by shares to get price per share."
  ],
